        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 758304,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The Nintendo Entertainment System (NES), initially released in Japan as the Family Computer (Famicom), revolutionized the home video game market after the 1983 crash. Developed by Nintendo, it succeeded their Color TV-Game line and competed with Sega's Master System. Designed with simplicity and affordability in mind, the NES featured detachable controllers and a distinctive front-loading cartridge design resembling a VCR, intended to distance it from the perception of a child's toy. \n\nThe system's success was fueled by a focus on third-party developer support and iconic games like *Super Mario Bros.*, *The Legend of Zelda*, *Dragon Quest*, and *Final Fantasy*, which became major franchises. While initially facing skepticism, the NES revitalized the American gaming industry and established Nintendo as a dominant force, pioneering a licensing model that continues to shape the industry today. A Disk System add-on was released in Japan, but ultimately proved less successful than cartridges."
          },
